\section*{Matroid Secretary Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 111; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that one sample per element gives a constant-factor matroid prophet rule even when the ordering adversary sees all values and the entire random seed, with guarantee $2^{-310}$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 111; 2026-09-23), reverse exposure turns samples into safe hidden-vector evidence. Geometric weight layers and nested flats guide greedy feasible selections, while rank expansion and nominal-path guards pay for all later choices uniformly over arrival orders.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 111; 2026-09-23). The manuscript also gives additional strength beyond the requested conclusion; that excess is not required for this problem's solved-claim label.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 111; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: one sample per element gives a constant-factor matroid prophet rule even when the ordering adversary sees all values and the entire random seed, with guarantee $2^{-310}$.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{MatroidProphet.lean}, \texttt{MatroidSecretary.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.MatroidProphet.Assigned.hidden\_vector}, \texttt{OAI.MatroidProphet.Assigned.one\_sample}, \texttt{OAI.MatroidProphet.Assigned.secretary}, \texttt{OAI.MatroidProphet.one\_sample}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

